      "content": "Apologies for the goblin metaphor. Let's dive into the technicalities of your Jenkins build issue. If your process is vanishing without an error code, it's indeed possible that the Out-Of-Memory (OOM) killer is terminating your Jenkins agent process. In a Dockerized environment, this can happen if your container is running out of memory and the Docker daemon decides to kill the process to free up resources.\n\nTo check if the OOM killer is involved, you can look at the system logs on the host machine where the Jenkins agent is running. On a Linux system, you can check the logs using the `dmesg` command or by looking at the `/var/log/syslog` file. Look for entries related to the OOM killer, which might indicate that it has been invoked.\n\nAdditionally, you can check the Docker daemon logs for any OOM killer-related messages. The location of these logs can vary depending on your system, but they are often found in `/var/log/docker.log` or by running `journalctl -u docker.service`.\n\nOther low-level system signals that could cause a process to exit silently include:\n\n1. SIGKILL: This signal is sent by the Linux kernel to terminate a process immediately. It's not",
